Layer 4: observation

Observation is how a scenario decides what the cluster did. There are
three channels: child logs, files on disk, and CLI output. The poll
rhythm and log scanning loop live in the shared runner (run/run.h).
Journal shapes and CLI verbs live in the drivers (driver/concord.h,
driver/weld.h). The suite executor (suite/exec.c) maps YAML
conditions onto those channels.

Channel 1: child log pipes

Every spawned binary has its stdout and stderr redirected into a
pipe. The check/ scenario reads chunks and searches for marker
strings. The YAML executor saves the same chunks to per-node log
files and greps the files. The two markers that carry nearly every
mesh verdict:

- "peers":N (sometimes with a space, "peers": N; scenarios check
  both). Means this node currently sees a full mesh.
- peer.seen / peer.lost. Means a peer arrived or vanished.

Specialist markers appear where the property needs them: pin
mismatch and peer key rotated in the key scenarios
(check/concord_pinalarm.c, check/concord_rotate.c), peer sync ok
plus a peer address in check/concord_killmidsync.c:178-180 (kill
exactly while syncing). On failure, check/ scenarios dump recent
error lines before cleaning up (grep '"level":"error"' ... | tail),
so the log that decided the run is visible. The suite executor runs
forensics through RunForensics (run/run.h:46) before the statedir
wipe instead. Two check/ scenarios (img, stop) also tee child
output to files under STATEDIR for post-mortem; the rest read pipes
only.

Channel 2: files on disk

Each node's home holds a journal (journal.jsonl), a config
(config.json), and stores. Check/ scenarios read them with shell
greps (they never write, except the key and journal surgery listed
below). The suite executor submits through concord_submit and reads
through concord_list_has, concord_assigned_anywhere, and
concord_mesh_count (driver/concord.h:71-123):

- AssignedNodeID per workload id: who is scheduled where. The
  assigned_anywhere helper (non-nil id in any journal) gates most
  workload scenarios.
- AssignmentEpoch: which reassignment generation a copy belongs
  to. Orphan, duel, update, and preempt verdicts compare epochs.
- Image: which container image a workload copy carries. Only the
  update scenario reads it.
- Node id from config.json: maps journal entries back to nodes,
  used to elect victims and leaders (lowest UUID) and to find owners.
  The executor uses the same mapping for kill plus restart
  targets.
- workload.instance.running: the image supply chain's end state.
  Only check/concord_img reads it.

The only writes to node state are the durability tests themselves:
deleting the Noise secret key (pinalarm), deleting journal plus kv
store (reinstall), and running node rotate-key (pinalarm,
rotate). Everything else is read-only.

Channel 3: CLI verbs

Check/ scenarios drive the system the way an operator does, with the
config home pointed at one node. The suite executor calls the same
verbs through driver/concord.h (concord_submit to submit,
concord_list_has and concord_mesh_count to read) and driver/weld.h
(weld_pub to send):

- workload run IMAGE: submit, parse Submitted workload uuid.
  Flags extend it: --memory for the no-fit and preemption
  scenarios.
- workload list: poll every 2 s, match the first 8 uuid characters.
  This is the standard "has it arrived everywhere" check.
- workload stop id: look for Stopped. Tombstone scenarios
  assert the stopped copy wins everywhere after reunion.
- workload update --image image id: only the update scenario.
- node list: count alive entries for mesh-size verdicts at scale.
- node rotate-key: only the key scenarios.
- Image supply (img_push, img_catalog, img_tags): only
  check/concord_img. Push the test image into every registry from
  inside its own namespace, read back catalogs and tags, submit, wait
  for a running instance.

Weld scenarios use weld pub (one publish from a node netns,
driver/weld.h:133) instead of workload run, and the observed
condition (weld log plus ROS log where a peer runs) instead of
journal grep. Same runner underneath.

The declared bypasses

There are two sanctioned bypasses. Both are explicit and documented,
never silent.

img_push deliberately avoids the mesh: it talks to each node's
registry over loopback inside that node's own namespace, because the
filesystem is shared across namespaces. No byte crosses the
simulated radio, so pushing never stalls behind a partition. This is
documented at the top of img/img.h.

The docker ROS-peer control path is the second sanctioned bypass
under the same policy. weld_ros_peer_start
(driver/weld.h:138-154) runs docker exec on the host against the
weld-distro container, with capture landing in the per-run
statedir so parallel runs stay apart. That control path never enters
a scenario netns and never crosses the simulated radio. Only weld
echo node traffic crosses the mesh. ROS output is observed through
the capture files, which is why the observed condition
(suite/exec.h:6-13) needs weld plus ROS logs where a peer runs.

What goes where

Runner (run/run.h): poll rhythm, log scanning loop, query
throttling, verdict plumbing, failure forensics. Driver
(driver/concord.h, driver/weld.h): journal shapes, CLI verbs,
marker strings, the bypass helpers. The suite executor
(suite/exec.c) maps YAML until, sustain, hold_s, and do
steps onto runner plus driver calls.
